//! # Actors
//!
//! Actors own state and run one handler at a time, including across suspension.
//! A call waits for completion. A void handler still returns a completion
//! outcome; use `match`, `?` or `handle` to deal with failure.
//!
//! ```hew
//! actor Counter {
//!     var count: i64 = 0;
//!     receive fn increment(n: i64) { count = count + n; }
//!     receive fn total() -> i64 { count }
//! }
//! fn main() {
//!     let counter = spawn Counter;
//!     counter.increment(42) handle error {
//!         println(f"increment failed: {error}");
//!         return;
//!     };
//!     match counter.total() {
//!         .Ok(value) => println(value),
//!         .Err(error) => println(f"query failed: {error}"),
//!     }
//!     stop(counter);
//!     stopped(counter);
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `fork counter.total()` creates a task; `await task` joins its result.
//! `mailbox(target, on_full: ...)` submits without waiting for completion and
//! refuses value-returning handlers. `policy(target, on_full: .Wait)` or the `.Reject` policy
//! controls admission for completion calls; a bare handle waits for room.
//!
//! Completion errors are `ActorError<E, Req>`. A rejecting completion view or
//! mailbox submission returns `Rejected(SendFailure<Req>)` only when the request
//! was definitely not admitted. Its message supports `retry()` and `to(target)`.
//! Other errors can leave remote admission or completion uncertain and do not
//! return a request to retry. Remote mailbox success acknowledges destination
//! admission, not handler completion.
//!
//! ## Isolation and backpressure
//!
//! Messages must satisfy [Send](language.ownership.html#send). Ordinary value data is snapshotted at the
//! actor boundary, while affine owners such as resources and stream halves
//! move. Actor state is accessed only by that actor's handlers, and each actor
//! runs one handler at a time.
//!
//! An actor without a `mailbox` declaration has unbounded capacity.
//! `mailbox 1024;` sets a bound and defaults to waiting for space. Declaration
//! policies include `drop_new`, `drop_old`, `fail`, and
//! `coalesce(key) fallback ...`. Coalescing applies only to submissions for a
//! unit-returning handler and replaces older queued work with the same handler
//! and key. Completion calls are never discarded or superseded.
//!
//! A `fails` handler returns `Failed(E)` to a completion caller. When submitted
//! through a mailbox view, its error faults the actor with its Display text.
//!
//! `stop(actor)` requests a graceful stop; `stopped(actor)` waits for terminal
//! cleanup. `terminate(actor)` requests cancellation. Inside an
//! actor, `self` is its handle and `self.field` accesses state. An actor's
//! handle has that actor's type `T`.
//!
//! ## Periodic handlers
//!
//! `#[every(duration)]` schedules a zero-argument handler through the actor's
//! normal mailbox. It starts with the actor and stops when the actor stops, so
//! periodic work never runs concurrently with another handler on that actor.
//!
//! ```hew
//! actor Heartbeat {
//!     var ticks: i64 = 0;
//!     #[every(1s)]
//!     receive fn tick() { ticks += 1; }
//!     receive fn count() -> i64 { ticks }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! A periodic handler has no parameters and unit success. On WASI its timer is
//! cooperative; see [target capabilities](language.targets.html).
//!
//! ## Lambda actors
//!
//! A lambda actor handles one message type. Its call waits for completion like
//! a named actor call, and its handle type is `actor(Message) -> Reply`.
//!
//! ```hew
//! fn main() {
//!     let double: actor(i64) -> i64 = actor |value: i64| -> i64 {
//!         value * 2
//!     };
//!     match double(21) {
//!         .Ok(value) => println(value),
//!         .Err(error) => println(f"worker failed: {error}"),
//!     }
//!     stop(double);
//!     stopped(double);
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `actor move |value| { ... }` requests transfer of captured owners. Ordinary
//! value captures already become independent actor state. Use a named actor for
//! several handlers, recursive protocols, trait implementation, or a named
//! supervisor child.
//!
//! ## Interceptors
//!
//! One `#[intercept]` method can inspect each receive delivery around the
//! handler. It consumes its `Call<Self>` exactly once with `proceed()` or
//! `refuse(reason)` and returns the resulting `Outcome<Self>`. Supervisor
//! interceptors wrap child interceptors from the outside in. Refusal is an
//! actor error for completion calls and does not crash the actor.
//!
//! Interceptors cover receive handlers, stream starts, and periodic ticks.
//! Lifecycle hooks, system messages, replies, and stream items bypass them.
//!
//! Supervised children and admitted remote actors also have the actor's own type.
//! A supervised reference follows its stable role through restarts. `is` compares
//! role identity for roles and exact incarnation identity for direct or imported
//! references; a role differs from its current occupant. `restarted(actor)` waits
//! only for an already pending role replacement to settle, returning immediately
//! for direct and imported references without asserting liveness.
